{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Of the foreigner you may exact it</b> — this is a positive commandment. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 15:3) from the Sifrei (see there). And its meaning is: the positive commandment is upon your brother; the foreigner you may exact, and not your brother — and a prohibition that comes by way of implication from a positive commandment is itself a positive commandment. And since they taught there (Sifrei 129) 'you shall not exact,' to establish upon it a negative commandment, they went back and taught 'of the foreigner you may exact' — this is a positive commandment, to say that one transgresses it both by a positive commandment and by a negative commandment. And thus they said there (Sifrei, Tiqqan 129): 'You may exact interest of the foreigner' (Deuteronomy 23:21) — a positive commandment. 'But you shall not exact interest of your brother' — this is a negative commandment. And it is as we explained: a positive commandment concerning your brother. And thus Rashi explained there: it does not mean that there is a commandment to lend to the foreigner at interest at all. And so it is proven in the Gemara in the chapter 'Which is interest?' (Bava Metzi'a 70a). And the Rav, Rabbi Moshe (Mishneh Torah, Laws of Lender and Borrower, ch. 1, hal. 2, and there ch. 5, hal. 1) made them both actual commandments: to exact from the foreigner and to lend to him at interest. He erred because of this language that is taught in the Sifrei, for there it is customary in many places: 'every clean bird you may eat' — a positive commandment; 'every swarming creature of the bird' — a negative commandment. And likewise they mentioned in the Sifra (Shemini, parashah 3:1) and in the Sifrei (see there): 'these are the animals which you may eat' — a positive commandment. And the matter is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Ramban refers to Maimonides (the Rav, Rabbi Moshe) by the common honorific 'הרב רבי משה'; the heading of the Mishneh Torah reference 'הל׳ מלוה ולוה' is rendered as 'Laws of Lender and Borrower.' The phrase 'לאו שבא מכלל עשה עשה' is a standard hermeneutical formula rendered here as 'a prohibition that comes by way of implication from a positive commandment is itself a positive commandment.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:50:19.646197764Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}